  {
    "role": "tool",
    "content": [
      {
        "cache_prompt": false,
        "type": "text",
        "text": "Here's the result of running `cat -n` on /home/pst/Documents/vhl/vhl_ana/virtual_hardware_laboratary/ana/skills/tsci_built_in_elements/resistor.md:\n     1\t# <resistor />\n     2\t\n     3\tA resistor element has two pins and is non-polar, meaning it doesn't matter if you place it on backwards (it resists electricity identically either way!)\n     4\t\n     5\tWhen specifying a resistor, you'll usually want to give it a footprint string such as `0402` or `0603` to indicate it's size. You can see the most popular resistor sizes for different power ratings at [jlcsearch](https://jlcsearch.tscircuit.com/resistors/list)\n     6\t\n     7\t```ts\n     8\texport default () => (\n     9\t<board width=\"10mm\" height=\"10mm\">  \n    10\t    <resistor    \n    11\t        name=\"R1\"    \n    12\t        footprint=\"0402\"    \n    13\t        resistance=\"1k\"  \n    14\t    />\n    15\t</board>)\n    16\t```\n    17\t\n    18\t\n    19\t## Pins\n    20\t\n    21\t| Pin # | Aliases | Description |\n    22\t| --- | --- | --- |\n    23\t| pin1 | left, pos | The left side pin in normal orientation |\n    24\t| pin2 | right, neg | The right side pin in normal orientation |\n    25\t\n    26\t## Tolerances\n    27\t\n    28\tResistors can be made to different tolerances. In particular, you might care about the following resistor characteristics:\n    29\t\n    30\t-   **tolerance** - a percentage given with a string, e.g. `tolerance=\"5%\"`. This specifies how accurate the resistance needs to be\n    31\t-   **powerRating** - a wattage e.g. \"5W\" indicating how much power can transfer through the resistor for normal operation\n    32\t-   **temperatureOperatingRange** - a string indicating the listed range for the resistor's operating temperature `\"-15F-150F\"`\n    33\t\n    34\t\n",
        "enable_truncation": true
      }
    ],
