  {
    "role": "tool",
    "content": [
      {
        "cache_prompt": false,
        "type": "text",
        "text": "Here's the result of running `cat -n` on /home/pst/Documents/vhl/vhl_ana/virtual_hardware_laboratary/ana/skills/tsci_built_in_elements/fuse.md:\n     1\t# <fuse />\n     2\t\n     3\t## Overview[\u7ab6\u72a0(#overview \"Direct link to Overview\")\n     4\t\n     5\tA `<fuse />` is a safety device that protects electrical circuits by interrupting current flow when it exceeds a predetermined threshold. Fuses are essential for preventing damage to components and circuits from overcurrent conditions.\n     6\t\n     7\tA fuse element has two pins and is typically non-polar, meaning it can be placed in either direction. When the current through the fuse exceeds its current rating, the fuse \"blows\" or opens the circuit to prevent damage to downstream components.\n     8\t\n     9\tWhen specifying a fuse, you'll need to provide the current rating and optionally the voltage rating and footprint. Common fuse types include surface-mount, through-hole, and cartridge fuses.\n    10\t\n    11\t```\n    12\texport default () => (<board width=\"10mm\" height=\"10mm\">  <fuse    name=\"F1\"    footprint=\"0603\"    currentRating=\"2A\"    voltageRating=\"32V\"  /></board>)\n    13\t```\n    14\t\n    15\t![PCB Circuit Preview](https://svg.tscircuit.com/?svg_type=pcb&code=H4sIAJsBqGcAAzXOOw7CMBAE0N6nWLlKqvwkKtsSDQegoDd4%2FZFiOzJr4PiEhFSjeZpi8LPkQmDQ6joTNC1IBQ0T96yLgXcw5CUf%2Bhg5eAzO078pBiBsfeKaAElHlPwy8K3ZnGkpIa3b%2FtRPOz5qKZjoqikkJ%2Fl43vmVZ9IOD57G2887xUS3XVCs%2FQLGbn9dowAAAA%3D%3D)![Schematic Circuit Preview](https://svg.tscircuit.com/?svg_type=schematic&code=H4sIAJsBqGcAAzXOOw7CMBAE0N6nWLlKqvwkKtsSDQegoDd4%2FZFiOzJr4PiEhFSjeZpi8LPkQmDQ6joTNC1IBQ0T96yLgXcw5CUf%2Bhg5eAzO078pBiBsfeKaAElHlPwy8K3ZnGkpIa3b%2FtRPOz5qKZjoqikkJ%2Fl43vmVZ9IOD57G2887xUS3XVCs%2FQLGbn9dowAAAA%3D%3D&simulation_experiment_id=simulation_experiment_0)![Pinout Circuit Preview](https://svg.tscircuit.com/?svg_type=pinout&code=H4sIAJsBqGcAAzXOOw7CMBAE0N6nWLlKqvwkKtsSDQegoDd4%2FZFiOzJr4PiEhFSjeZpi8LPkQmDQ6joTNC1IBQ0T96yLgXcw5CUf%2Bhg5eAzO078pBiBsfeKaAElHlPwy8K3ZnGkpIa3b%2FtRPOz5qKZjoqikkJ%2Fl43vmVZ9IOD57G2887xUS3XVCs%2FQLGbn9dowAAAA%3D%3D)![3D Circuit Preview](https://svg.tscircuit.com/?svg_type=3d&format=png&png_width=800&png_height=600&show_infinite_grid=true&background_color=%23ffffff&code=H4sIAJsBqGcAAzXOOw7CMBAE0N6nWLlKqvwkKtsSDQegoDd4%2FZFiOzJr4PiEhFSjeZpi8LPkQmDQ6joTNC1IBQ0T96yLgXcw5CUf%2Bhg5eAzO078pBiBsfeKaAElHlPwy8K3ZnGkpIa3b%2FtRPOz5qKZjoqikkJ%2Fl43vmVZ9IOD57G2887xUS3XVCs%2FQLGbn9dowAAAA%3D%3D)\n    16\t\n    17\t## Pins[\u7ab6\u72a0(#pins \"Direct link to Pins\")\n    18\t\n    19\t| Pin # | Aliases | Description |\n    20\t| --- | --- | --- |\n    21\t| pin1 | left, in | The input side pin in normal orientation |\n    22\t| pin2 | right, out | The output side pin in normal orientation |\n    23\t\n    24\t## Properties[\u7ab6\u72a0(#properties \"Direct link to Properties\")\n    25\t\n    26\t| Property | Type | Required | Description | Example |\n    27\t| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n    28\t| `currentRating` | `number | string` | Yes | The current rating at which the fuse will blow | `\"2A\"`, `1.5`, `\"500mA\"` |\n    29\t| `voltageRating` | `number | string` | No | The maximum voltage the fuse can safely interrupt | `\"32V\"`, `250` |\n    30\t| `schShowRatings` | `boolean` | No | Whether to display the ratings on the schematic symbol | `true`, `false` |\n    31\t| `schOrientation` | `string` | No | The orientation of the fuse in the schematic | `\"left\"`, `\"right\"`, `\"up\"`, `\"down\"` |\n    32\t\n    33\t## Current Ratings[\u7ab6\u72a0(#current-ratings \"Direct link to Current Ratings\")\n    34\t\n    35\tFuses are available in a wide range of current ratings from microamps to hundreds of amperes. Common current ratings include:\n    36\t\n    37\t-   **Low current**: 100mA, 250mA, 500mA, 1A\n    38\t-   **Medium current**: 2A, 3A, 5A, 10A\n    39\t-   **High current**: 15A, 20A, 30A and above\n    40\t\n    41\tThe current rating should be chosen based on the normal operating current of your circuit, typically 1.5-2x the expected current to avoid nuisance blowing.\n    42\t\n    43\t## Voltage Ratings[\u7ab6\u72a0(#voltage-ratings \"Direct link to Voltage Ratings\")\n    44\t\n    45\tThe voltage rating indicates the maximum voltage the fuse can safely interrupt when it blows. Common voltage ratings include:\n    46\t\n    47\t-   **Low voltage**: 32V, 63V (for automotive and low-voltage applications)\n    48\t-   **AC mains**: 125V, 250V (for household and industrial applications)\n    49\t-   **High voltage**: 600V and above (for industrial and power applications)\n    50\t\n    51\t## Schematic Display Options[\u7ab6\u72a0(#schematic-display-options \"Direct link to Schematic Display Options\")\n    52\t\n    53\tThe `schShowRatings` property controls whether the current and voltage ratings are displayed on the schematic symbol. This can be helpful for documentation but may clutter dense schematics.\n    54\t\n    55\tThe `schOrientation` property allows you to control how the fuse is oriented in the schematic layout for better routing and readability.\n",
        "enable_truncation": true
      }
    ],
